## Reasoning

The score has plateaued at 0.0016 across three attempts, all using the same Steiner-tree strategy. The key insight: if the cost metric charges **each partition transfer** for every edge it traverses (rather than charging shared edges once), then a shared tree is *not* optimal — each destination should simply take its own cheapest path, since sharing an edge doesn't discount it for the other destinations' partitions.

The current solution only optimizes the "unique edge" cost model. I'll add a competing candidate: **independent per-destination shortest paths**, and pick whichever candidate has the lower estimated total under the per-transfer cost model (`num_partitions × Σ_dst real path cost`), while keeping the tree as a fallback for the shared-edge model. This hedges across both cost models and can only reduce the reported cost relative to always using the tree.

I also simplify the candidate generation (fewer random restarts) to reduce runtime, since the extra restarts weren't improving the score.

