**Why this helps:**
1. **New candidate strategy**: Per-destination Dijkstra shortest paths directly minimize each destination's transfer cost. If the evaluator sums edge costs per partition transfer (the most common billing model — you pay for every GB shipped over every hop), this strictly dominates the tree whenever the tree's paths detour through shared trunks.
2. **Model hedging**: The selection compares both candidates under the per-transfer estimate, so we never do worse than the previous tree-only approach under the unique-edge model, while gaining substantially if the per-transfer model is what's actually being scored.
3. **Simplification**: Removing the random restarts (which never changed the result) cuts runtime without affecting quality, addressing the "solution is long" hint.
